  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">From Edmonton to international competition</p>
    <h2>A 400-metre athlete developing on the Canadian pathway</h2>
    <p>Dianna Proctor is an Edmonton athlete who competes for the University of Guelph and the Edmonton Royals. Athletics Canada listed her primary event as the 400 metres when naming her to its 2026 NextGen European Tour, and she has also represented Canada in relay competition.</p>
    <p>At the 2026 World Athletics Relays in Gaborone, Proctor was selected for Canada's women's and mixed 4×400 metre pools. In the mixed relay, the Canadian squad of Nathan George, Ella Clayton, Ryder Rattee and Proctor ran 3:12.43, setting a Canadian record and securing qualification for the 2027 World Athletics Championships.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">2026 collegiate season</p><h2>U SPORTS 300 m champion</h2><p>At the 2026 U SPORTS Track & Field Championships, Proctor won the women's 300 metres. She also contributed to Guelph's gold-medal 4×400 metre relay and silver-medal 4×200 metre relay as the Gryphon women won the national team title.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Why the partnership fits</p>
    <h2>Hydration belongs in the routine, not in the credit for the result</h2>
    <p>Sprint training and international competition involve repeated decisions about fluids, food, heat, travel and recovery. That makes hydration relevant to Proctor's real environment and gives the partnership a useful context.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate does not present Dianna's medals, records or selections as proof that the product caused those performances. Athlete achievement is the result of the athlete's training, coaching, health, recovery and many other factors. Her experience can illustrate a hydration routine without replacing controlled evidence.</p>
  </article>
